{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Verified the clean way in: installing with `cledger` hidden from PATH makes the installer fall back to absolute paths, baking the worktree build into the plugin. No npm-link surgery, no branch switching, nothing touched in your main checkout.\n\n## Step 0 — the trap, so you don't hit it\n\n`cledger` on your PATH is **0.16.0** (npm-linked to the main checkout). It has no `hook opencode`. If you run `cledger install opencode` the normal way, the plugin bakes in the bare string `\"cledger\"`, opencode resolves that to 0.16.0 at runtime, it errors — and because the plugin discards stderr, **you see absolutely nothing**. It'll look like the plugin never fired.\n\n## Step 1 — install the plugin, pointed at the branch build\n\n```sh\nW=/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/opencode-adapter\nPATH=/usr/bin:/bin $(which node) \"$W/dist/cli.js\" install opencode\ngrep '^const COMMAND' ~/.config/opencode/plugin/cledger.js\n```\n\nThat last line must show an absolute path ending in `opencode-adapter/dist/cli.js`. If it shows `[\"cledger\",...]`, stop — you'd be testing 0.16.0.\n\n## Step 2 — the highest-value test: pin the real event shape\n\nThis is the one open question in the code. The plugin currently guesses the session-id key across four names because I couldn't capture a live `session.idle`. Drop this probe in alongside it:\n\n```sh\ncat > ~/.config/opencode/plugin/_probe.js <<'EOF'\nexport const server = async () => {\n  const fs = await import(\"node:fs\");\n  const LOG = process.env.HOME + \"/opencode-events.jsonl\";\n  return {\n    event: async ({ event }) => {\n      if (!event || !String(event.type).startsWith(\"session.\")) return;\n      try { fs.appendFileSync(LOG, JSON.stringify(event) + \"\\n\"); } catch {}\n    },\n  };\n};\nEOF\n```\n\nAfter a turn (step 3), read it:\n\n```sh\ngrep '\"session.idle\"' ~/opencode-events.jsonl | head -1 | python3 -m json.tool\n```\n\nWhatever key holds the session id there is the answer. Tell me and I'll pin it and delete the fallback — that closes a roadmap item.\n\n## Step 3 — actually drive the TUI\n\nUse a scratch repo first so you're not writing test events into cledger's own ledger:\n\n```sh\nmkdir -p /tmp/octest && cd /tmp/octest && git init -b main .\necho \"hello from the ledger test\" > note.txt && git add -A && git commit -m init\nopencode\n```\n\nPlugins load at **startup**, so this must be a fresh `opencode`, not one already running. Then in the TUI ask something that exercises text, reasoning and a tool call — e.g. *\"read note.txt and tell me exactly what it says\"* — and wait for it to finish (that's when `session.idle` fires). Then send a **second** message, so you're testing incremental capture rather than one-shot.\n\n## Step 4 — check it landed\n\n```sh\ncd /tmp/octest\ncledger conversations      # expect one opencode:<ses_...> row with a model name\ncledger log                # your prompts, the reply, thinking blocks, tool calls\n```\n\nCapture is detached and runs after idle, so give it a second or two. Things worth confirming by eye: your prompt appears as a **human** turn with your git identity; the model's reasoning shows as `thinking`; the tool call shows `tool_use` **and** `tool_result` in one turn; `producer.model`/`provider` are set (`cledger log --json | head -1`).\n\n## Step 5 — the cases most likely to break\n\n- **Idempotency:** `cledger log --json | wc -l`, then `cledger capture opencode --all`, then count again. Must be identical.\n- **Subagent skip:** ask for something that spawns a subagent (`@explore`-style). The parent's `task` tool call should be captured; the child session should produce no separate conversation.\n- **Drift:** run `cledger capture opencode --all` manually and watch stderr. If it warns about preserved unrecognized part types, that's a real finding — those are the `file`/`agent`/`snapshot`/`todo` types I deliberately left unclassified, and it tells me which ones actually occur.\n- **Attachments:** drag a file into the TUI prompt. I have no sample of a `file` part, so this is the most likely source of a drift warning.\n\n## Step 6 — if nothing shows up\n\nThe plugin swallows all output by design, so reproduce it in the open. Grab the session id and run the hook by hand with exactly the payload the plugin sends:\n\n```sh\nopencode session list --format json -n 3\ncd /tmp/octest\necho '{\"session_id\":\"ses_XXXX\",\"cwd\":\"'$PWD'\"}' | \\\n  $(which node) \"$W/dist/cli.js\" hook opencode\n```\n\nThat prints everything the plugin hides. If *that* works but the TUI path doesn't, the problem is the plugin/event side, and step 2's log will show whether `session.idle` fired at all.\n\n## Step 7 — cleanup\n\n```sh\nrm -f ~/.config/opencode/plugin/_probe.js ~/opencode-events.jsonl\nrm -rf /tmp/octest\n```\n\nLeave `cledger.js` in place if you want to keep capturing, but note it points into the worktree — after you merge PR #2 and rebuild the main checkout, re-run `cledger install opencode` normally so it goes back to the bare `cledger` command.\n\nOne caveat on what this proves: steps 3–5 verify the plugin fires, the payload reaches the hook, and conversion is right end-to-end. They don't verify behavior under a `session revert` — that's the positional-`seq` limitation on the roadmap, and if you want to poke it, revert a session in the TUI and then re-run `cledger capture opencode --all`; expect duplicate turns after the revert point rather than an error.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"worktree-opencode-adapter","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/opencode-adapter","head":"c70ed9d897dd2ec353154a754a100bd72c311c79","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:fe9cea6b-80c2-4e6e-bff4-aa88447380f6","seq":485},"id":"ev1-f3a7228659cfbb943b7a4a52e8d005a6d64f0fbab873dd819d6485